From 0ea3cdd4761acdab96b0b0fd262431cd2f82e9e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:29:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Currency switcher on 20 calculators, and the copy to match it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The rupee sign was hard-coded into calculators whose surrounding text
was written for a US or UK reader. A visitor working out a contractor's
markup, a break-even point or a flooring estimate got a currency they do
not use, on a page that otherwise addressed them directly. That
mismatch, more than any single wrong number, is what makes a page read
as assembled rather than written.

Each of these 20 now carries the switcher from the last patch: the
symbol next to every field and every result follows the reader's choice,
digit grouping follows with it (toLocaleString had "en-IN" pinned in
several files, so an American reader saw 12,34,567), and the choice
carries across the site.

The static copy could not be switched — the FAQ text is also the source
of the FAQ structured data, so it has to stay fixed or the schema and
the visible answer drift apart. It is written in USD, the default, and
the worked examples had to be rebuilt rather than relabelled:

- Amortization opened on a "$50 lakh at 9% for 20 years" example. It now
  works through a $350,000 mortgage at 6.5% over 30 years, with the
  payment schedule and the extra-payment table recomputed from that loan
  rather than relabelled.
- College Cost was an Indian page in an American costume: B.Tech and
  MBBS fee tables, SBI and HDFC education loans, the National
  Scholarship Portal, "70%+ in 12th". Both tables are replaced with US
  institution types, and the aid answers now describe the FAFSA, Pell
  Grants and federal versus private loans.
- ROI, CAGR, APY, Loan Comparison and Loan Prepayment each had lakh and
  crore figures; those are rewritten at realistic US amounts.

Invoice Generator was on the list and has been taken off it: CGST, SGST,
IGST, HSN codes — it is an Indian GST tool, and it belongs with the
other Indian instruments under /in/, in rupees.

